.docname {Package CLI}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

`@bakingrl/create-plugin` provides two commands:

- `create-bakingrl-plugin` scaffolds trusted v4 packages.
- `bakingrl-plugin` validates, inspects, packs, signs, and locally installs
  trusted v4 packages.

## Templates

```sh
create-bakingrl-plugin my-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-native --template native-sidecar-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-platform --template platform-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-contributor --template contributor-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-content --template content-pack-plugin
```

Available templates:

- `extension-plugin`: extension host entry plus a webview contribution.
- `native-sidecar-plugin`: extension host entry plus a Rust JSON-RPC stdio
  sidecar.
- `platform-plugin`: extension host service, webview, public resource,
  and extension point for other packages.
- `contributor-plugin`: dependency-backed contribution with a service,
  and public resource.
- `content-pack-plugin`: resource-only package that contributes public JSON and
  SVG content to another package.

Generated packages declare `schemaVersion: "bakingrl.plugin/4"`, and
`bakingrlApi: "2.3.0"`. They include an explicit empty permission declaration
and an author-owned `marketplace/listing.json` ready to edit.

## Validate

```sh
bakingrl-plugin validate .
```

Validation checks:

- manifest schema, id, name, version, `bakingrlApi` compatibility, runtime,
  permissions, dependencies, contributes, and host-owned settings;
- exact or single-terminal-wildcard bus, registry, and storage patterns;
- structured HTTP, WebSocket, and listen endpoints;
- relative storage paths without URI prefixes, absolute paths, `..`, or
  backslashes;
- built JavaScript entries for extension host and declared webviews;
- complete `surface` webviews with immutable capability declarations;
- sidecar command paths and `jsonrpc-stdio` declarations;
- sidecar health-check declarations when present;
- package-local paths for declared contribution schemas, resources, webviews,
  and runtime entries;
- dependency-backed cross-package contributions;
- local references from extension points and contributions to declared
  services and resources;
- absence of bundled Node runtime binaries such as `node`, `node.exe`, or
  `bin/node*`.

Current validation accepts `contributes.extensionPoints`,
`contributes.contributions`, `contributes.resources`, and
`contributes.webviews`. A contribution targeting another package must declare a
matching top-level dependency. Runtime API validation accepts manifests in the
`2.3.x` window, with `2.3.0` as the minimum, and rejects every other target.

## Doctor

```sh
bakingrl-plugin doctor .
```

`doctor` prints a JSON summary of `bakingrlApi`, permissions, dependencies, sidecars,
contribution ids, resources, webviews, and build entries.

## Inspect

```sh
bakingrl-plugin inspect .
```

`inspect` prints the normalized v4 manifest surface used by the host:
`schemaVersion`, `bakingrlApi`, `permissions`, `runtime`, and `contributes`.

## Author Listing

Every generated package includes `marketplace/listing.json`. Validate its
package id, text limits, repository, media URLs, screenshots, and links with:

```sh
bakingrl-plugin validate-listing .
```

The listing is display metadata owned by the package author. It is not a trust
decision and it is not the signed marketplace catalogue.

## Pack

```sh
npm run build
bakingrl-plugin pack .
```

`pack` validates the package, writes `manifest.hashes.json`, and creates a
`.brlp` bundle in `dist-bundles/`.

To sign while packing:

```sh
bakingrl-plugin keygen bakingrl-signing-key.json
bakingrl-plugin pack . --sign bakingrl-signing-key.json
```

Do not commit signing keys, signatures, hashes, bundles, or generated build
outputs unless a release process explicitly requires them.

## Prepare A Submission

After producing a signed release bundle, emit review input with:

```sh
bakingrl-plugin prepare-submission . \
  --developer example \
  --bundle-url https://github.com/example/package/releases/download/v1.0.0/package.brlp \
  --listing-url https://raw.githubusercontent.com/example/package/v1.0.0/marketplace/listing.json \
  --platform windows-x64 \
  --output submission.json
```

The `bakingrl.marketplace-submission/1` document records the package version,
Runtime API, author listing location, artifact hash, signing public key, and
declared permissions. It deliberately contains no review status, approval, or
`bakingrl.marketplace/2` catalogue structure; those belong to the marketplace
repository.

## Local Install

```sh
bakingrl-plugin install-local .
```

This copies the package into the BakingRL app data package directory. Use it
after `npm run build` when testing a package in the host app.
